  "insights": [
    "Customers spend about 58.87% of the money they receive (debits INR 84.7 M vs credits INR 143.9 M), i.e. a net inflow of INR 59.2 M across the nine months.",
    "'Rent' is the largest spending category (27.63% of debit value) and the top three categories together account for 58.0%. Rent is a fixed monthly payment, so most discretionary spending sits in Shopping, Travel and ATM cash - the natural focus for card offers and partnerships.",
    "Spending peaks in 2026-05 (INR 10.12 M) and is lowest in 2026-07 (INR 8.71 M); campaigns and liquidity planning can follow this pattern.",
    "Excluding rent, the top 5% of debits (>= INR 11,162) carry 31.36% of discretionary debit value, so a small set of transactions deserves extra monitoring.",
    "K-Means splits the 300 accounts into three tiers; the High-value tier is 29.0% of accounts but 46.95% of spending - the group to prioritise for relationship management.",
    "9 accounts went below zero at least once (0.09% of transactions) - candidates for overdraft alerts and low-balance nudges.",
    "Fraud is rare (2.36% of debits); Random Forest gave the best F1 (0.475) with the highest precision (0.553), while Logistic Regression caught more fraud (recall 0.871) at the cost of many false alarms - the bank must choose the balance between missed fraud and review workload.",
    "Unsupervised Isolation Forest ranks fraud above normal activity (ROC AUC 0.855) but its fixed 3% cut-off flagged 642 debits and caught only 16.7% of fraud, so it is best used to prioritise manual review when labels are unavailable, not as a stand-alone detector."
  ],
  "conclusion": "The analysis of 25,513 cleaned transactions from 300 accounts shows regular, salary-driven inflows and category-wise spending. K-Means produced three interpretable customer tiers (silhouette 0.288); Isolation Forest and Random Forest both detected unusual transactions, with Random Forest performing best on F1. Results should be re-validated on real bank data."
}